"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"Originally reported by: **Yann Dirson (BitBucket: [ydirson](http://bitbucket.org/ydirson), GitHub: @ydirson?)**\n\n---\n\nI have written a small pylint plugin, stored in my project as Overlord/pylint.py.\nFirst, I had to blindly try to find out I had to use \" load-plugins=Overlord/pylint\" and not\n\"load-plugins=Overlord.pylint\", as would be expected from the \"as comma separated values of python modules names\". That gives a cryptic \"ImportError: No module named interfaces\".\n\nThen I can't seem to configure pylint to find the module :\n- default sys.path includes \".\", so \"import Overlord.pylint\" works from the interpreter\n- \"PYTHONPATH=$PWD pylint\" finds the plugin\n- just \"pylint\" does not find it\n\nIt is again not very intuitive to have a behaviour that's not consistent with python. strace reveals that the directory search nearly follows sys.path, but looks in /usr/bin/ first instead of cwd - is that a misinterpretation of '' as sys.path element ?\n\nThen I stumbled on http://stackoverflow.com/questions/1899436/pylint-unable-to-import-error-how-to-set-pythonpath which looks like it could be used as a workaround, but even appending an absolute hardcoded version of $PWD does not result in anything. Introducing a typo in the init-hook does not even produce an error, in fact : is it even run ?\n\n---\n- Bitbucket: https://bitbucket.org/logilab/pylint/issue/166\n","closed_by":{"login":"pylint-bot","id":16198247,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MTk4MjQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16198247?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot","html_url":"https://github.com/pylint-bot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot/fol